include "../../linux.mereo"

-- REJECTED on purpose: a nested loop that takes the enclosing loop's counter.
--
-- Every scalar in mereo is visible everywhere. That is what lets a scope see
-- its surroundings without plumbing anything through ports -- and it is why
-- reaching for a fresh counter inside a nested scope silently takes the one
-- the outer loop is counting on. `inner` means to count to three; it resets
-- `i`, which `outer` is using to count to four, and neither ever finishes.
--
-- C gives each block its own `i` and warns at `-Wshadow`. mereo cannot: the
-- name IS the same name. So the mistake has to be caught rather than scoped
-- away, and the shape that is decidable is narrow -- two loops, one inside the
-- other, bound by the same name, with that name RESET between them.
--
-- Sharing a name is not enough to be wrong. `examples/head.mereo` counts
-- newlines across blocks with an accumulator that the inner loop advances and
-- both loops stop on, which is correct and must keep compiling. The difference
-- is the reset: an advance carries the count forward, a reset throws it away.
--
-- Found by writing 357 lines of mereo for `exam/` and hitting it: the read
-- loop's index was reused as a parse counter, and nothing said so.

program goes
  word is 8 bytes
  i is 0
  t is 0
  terminal is already linux.file (descriptor is 1)
  outer goes
    leave outer when i >= 4
    inner goes
      i is 0
      count goes
        leave count when i >= 3
        t is t + 1
        i is i + 1
        repeat count
      end
    end
    i is i + 1
    repeat outer
  end
  [word + 0 : 8] is t
  terminal.write (buffer is word, count is 8)
end
